A||N||7||Lou Christopher||T 125 lock up||||1||
Z||000000||Lou Christopher||08-10-2000||12:07 PM||||I am slowly trying to correct a multitude of problems with the 64 SE 350 V8 that I purchased.  It is a carburated engine, no computer.  I would like to wire a manual lock up for the toque convertor.  I looked at the wiring, it is a four connector plug with only two wires.  One is 12 volts when the key is on. I do not know what the other is.  I know there is a 3 rd gear switch in the trans but I would assume it will close itself. Does anyone know how to wire the switch to manually lock up the convertor.  Lou<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||08-10-2000||03:32 PM||||You need to work out a couple things. <P>Thing 1 is the brake switch. hook a test light to the wire that is always hot with the key on and hit the brake pedal. The line should go dead. If it doesn't you can't wire the switch manually. <B>The Brake Switch MUST work or you'll have nothing but problems.</B> Dangerous problems.<P>Thing 2 is to test the operation of the third gear switch. This switch must also work or the TCC will try to actiave as soon as you take your foot off the brake when stopped. This is not a good thing. (Same effect as dumping a clutch every time you take your foot off the brake. You WILL brake the tranny like that.) I imagine your's is ok but you need to test it to be safe.<P>To test the third gear switch connect the test light between the "extra" wire and ground. You'll need long leads for this. Put the light where it can be seen while driving and have a passenger watch it. When the car goes into third the light should come on. (It may be dim because of TCC solinoid resistance. You may have to use a volt meter instead of a lamp.)<P><B>BOTH switches Must work. Without both switches you cannot wire the manual switch without damaging the tranny.</B><BR> <BR>The "dead" wire be a ground. The ECM used to ground that wire to activate the TCC. Your new switch will replace the ECM. Best way to do that is trace the wire to the old ECM connector and extend the lead as needed.<P>Manual locking presents several problems. <P>You want to avoid using the manual lock except for highway driving. You don't want the tranny locking as soon as it hits third gear which is exactly what it will do with the switch on. If the TC locks at the same time you hit third you'll likely tear up the tranny. (You also lose some performance that way.)<P>If you get into heavy traffic you'll want to leave it unlocked, even if the tranny stays in third.<P>If I was doing this I'd find a way to make a speed sensor for the circuit. That way the TC will never lock below a specific speed. The ECM cutoff was no lock below 35. In your case I'd make it no lock below 50, and add Transmission Exchange co's TCC delay unit. That would return autolocking in the safest way posible.<P>A speed sensor would be fairly easy to do. There's a 2000ppm feed back to the ECM connector from the dash. There's any number of ways to make that feed close a switch at a particular speed or above. <P>Before anyone asks, Yes I could do it, No I won't. I simply can't take any new projects for quite some time. I'm way behind just fixing broken things. It would have to be worth a good chunk of cash before I'd be able to even consider it. Are there allot of V8's that need it? My impresion is that most V8 Fiero's are stick.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Lou Christopher||08-11-2000||08:59 AM||||Theorge,  Thanks for the info.  I think I get the picture.  I do not think I am enough of a electronics person to builf a speed sensor switch.  I will probably just put a manual in for the time being and try to remember to shut if off after cruising. The only thing I was not sure of was the Transxchange delay you recommended.  Went to their website but still do not understand the need. If I eventually get a speed switch what does the delay do?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-11-2000||11:53 AM||||It will do for the speed switch about the same thing it does if the ECM was present. It helps keep the TCC from egaging untill the speed is over the limit for a couple seconds. This prevents the TC from locking and unlocking constantly when you are close to the lock limit. It's easier to just use the delay unit than build it into the switch.<P>you don't need the delay unit with the manual switch. I'm not sure it would work right anyway.<P><B>WARNING!!!!! I got to thinking about this more later. You MUST use a volt meter when testing the third gear switch!</B> A test lamp may allow enough current to flow for the TC to lock as soon as the TG switch closes. You don't want that when testing.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-11-2000||01:35 PM||||<B>HOLD OFF ON THIS PROJECT!!!!!!!!!!</B><P>I've figured out a very cheap, very easy, to build way of doing this that solves some of the safety and reliability problems. It should cost less than $10 to build it and anyone who has basic soldering skills should be able to make one. It's not a fully automatic fix but it should work very well. It will be far safer than using a simple togle switch.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Lou Christopher||08-12-2000||09:16 AM||||Would be very interested in anything you come up with. Really appreciate your help.  At that price range would be glad to compensate you for design since I am presuming you have no use for the device.  to be honest I would not be driveing an auto if my left leg would allow be to drive a standard.  At 62 with a beat up body you have to make some compromises||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-12-2000||07:45 PM||||I now know how to do this and how to hook it up. It's pretty easy. I need to do a bit of testing. It will be awhile before I can do that. ((main thing I want to know is if it's really as easy as it looks. Almost Nothing with Fiero is this easy.))||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-12-2000||08:46 PM||||As long as the original TC lockup harness to the transmission is intact then this is as easy as it's looking. If the original harness has been ripped out then you're going to have to restore it.<P>I have to test it a bit but it's not major. I'm not ripping my car apart to test it, but I have an alternate method that should tell me it works. <P>The whole thing will live under the console behind the ALDL cover. You could put the button on the ALDL cover or any place else you want.||||reg||1
